        "content": "Exchange rates between sovereign currencies reflect the relative economic health, productivity, and monetary policy trajectory of the issuing nations. In macroeconomic theory, exchange rate parity is modeled through Uncovered Interest Parity (UIP) and Purchasing Power Parity (PPP). In practical financial markets entering 2027, exchange rates trade on **interest rate expectation differentials**.\n\nCentral banks (including the US Federal Reserve, the European Central Bank, the Bank of England, and the Bank of Japan) adjust benchmark policy rates to fulfill their legal mandates of price stability and employment. When government statistical agencies release macroeconomic data, market participants immediately update their probability models regarding future central bank actions.\n\nConsider the causal transmission chain of a Tier-1 release:\n1. **The Economic Print**: The US Bureau of Labor Statistics releases the Consumer Price Index (CPI), showing core inflation running 0.4% above consensus expectations.\n2. **Bond Market Repricing**: Institutional fixed income desks immediately sell short-term US Treasury notes. Two-year Treasury yields surge by 14 basis points as traders price in an increased probability of Federal Reserve rate hikes or delayed rate cuts.\n3. **Foreign Exchange Capital Flows**: Global investment funds shift capital out of lower-yielding currencies (such as the Japanese Yen or Swiss Franc) into higher-yielding US Dollar money market instruments to capture the yield spread.\n4. **Spot Market Directional Trend**: Spot EUR/USD drops 65 pips over the subsequent 45 minutes as institutional selling absorbs retail bids.\n\nHowever, trading this dynamic successfully requires understanding that markets trade on **deviation from consensus**, not absolute numbers. If an economic indicator shows robust growth but institutional forecasts had already priced in exceptional growth, the currency will frequently drop on the news. This phenomenon is known as 'buying the rumor, selling the news'.\n\nUnderstand the specific central bank mandates and sovereign bond yield drivers behind each currency in our [major forex currency pairs and economic indicators analysis](/blog/major-forex-currency-pairs-economic-indicators)."
